The Socratic Machine

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : Claude, Deepseek 

Abstract

We present the Socratic Machine, a cryptographic protocol for privacy-preserving, verifiable contribution assessment and resource allocation in decentralised coordination systems. The protocol combines three zk‑SNARK circuits—task completion, impact assessment, and aggregation—to enable participants to prove that they have completed tasks and that their impact scores are computed honestly, without revealing the underlying private data. A nullifier mechanism based on the Poseidon hash function guarantees that each secret key can be used at most once per epoch, providing key‑limited Sybil resistance. Contributions are aggregated off‑chain and verified on‑chain through a single Groth16 proof that enforces nullifier uniqueness and computes the epoch average. A softmax‑based resource allocation function (the PRI mechanism) distributes a fixed pool of resources according to the relative impact of each participant, with analytically bounded sensitivity that limits the profitability of score manipulation. We analyse incentives as an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma and prove that cooperation is sustainable as a subgame perfect equilibrium under grim trigger when the discount factor exceeds one‑half. The overall system is proven sound under standard cryptographic assumptions, and we discuss implementation trade‑offs, security margins, and extensions toward full Sybil resistance via external identity‑binding oracles.

---

Introduction

Decentralised autonomous organisations, open‑source ecosystems, and collaborative knowledge networks face a persistent challenge: how to measure and reward contributions fairly without a central authority, while preserving the privacy of individual participants. Traditional reputation systems rely on persistent identities, which link activity across contexts and expose sensitive metadata. Conversely, purely anonymous systems suffer from Sybil attacks, where a single entity creates many identities to capture a disproportionate share of rewards. A ideal contribution‑aware system should (i) verifiably attest to the completion of specific tasks, (ii) produce a tamper‑proof impact score derived from private metrics, (iii) aggregate many such scores into a global allocation while preventing double‑submission, and (iv) do all of the above without leaking information about who did what.

The Socratic Machine addresses this quadruple challenge by unifying zero‑knowledge proofs, cryptographic accumulators, and game‑theoretic incentives into a single protocol. At its core, three non‑interactive zero‑knowledge circuits—each proved independently and verified on‑chain—enable participants to earn impact scores. The task completion circuit confirms that a participant has performed a designated task and outputs a nullifier derived from the participant’s secret key and a task identifier, allowing the system to track activity without linking it to a long‑term identity. The impact assessment circuit attests that a set of private quality metrics, weighted by a domain‑specific scoring function, yields a normalised impact score in [0,1]. The aggregation circuit collects the nullifiers and scores from all active participants in an epoch, enforces their strict ordering to guarantee that no secret key is used twice, and outputs the epoch’s mean impact and a binding commitment. Crucially, sorting the nullifier list is performed off‑chain by the prover, so the circuit only verifies monotonicity, reducing on‑chain constraints from O(N\log N) to O(N).

Our design builds on well‑established cryptographic primitives. We instantiate the zk‑SNARK with the Groth16 proving system over the BN254 pairing‑friendly curve, using the Poseidon hash for Merkle tree commitments and nullifier generation because of its low arithmetic complexity. The resource allocation function—the Proportional Resource by Impact (PRI) mechanism—applies a softmax with temperature \beta to the set of impact scores, distributing a fixed total resource R. We prove that the sensitivity of any participant’s allocation to changes in their own score is bounded by \beta P_i, making small undetected inflations unrewarding. This local fairness property is complemented by a global incentive analysis: we model the repeated interaction among participants as an infinitely repeated Prisoner’s Dilemma with discount factor \delta, and we show that the cooperative equilibrium (all participants exert honest effort) is subgame perfect under grim trigger whenever \delta \ge 1/2, with the threshold shifting predictably if the cost of cooperation changes.

We emphasise what the system does not provide: full Sybil resistance. The nullifier construction only prevents a single secret key from being used more than once per epoch. An adversary can still create arbitrarily many distinct keys. Achieving complete Sybil resistance requires an external mechanism—such as proof‑of‑personhood, stake‑based registration, or biometric oracles—that limits the number of keys a real‑world entity can control. Our protocol supplies the cryptographic infrastructure on which such identity‑binding can be layered.

The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. Section 1 recalls the algebraic and geometric preliminaries of finite fields and elliptic curves. Section 2 presents the R1CS and Groth16 proving system. Section 3 describes the Poseidon hash, Merkle trees, and pairing details. Section 4 specifies the three Socratic Machine circuits in detail. Section 5 analyses the nullifier construction and its Sybil‑resistance properties. Section 6 derives the softmax allocation and its sensitivity bound. Section 7 models the repeated game and proves the cooperative equilibrium. Section 8 compares the aggregation circuit’s complexity with an in‑circuit sorting alternative. Section 9 proves the end‑to‑end soundness of the system. Section 10 introduces a supplementary resilience metric. Appendices A–H provide full constraint counts, pairing computations, extended security proofs, and implementation guidance. Together, these components form a rigorous foundation for privacy‑preserving, verifiable contribution measurement in decentralised governance.

1. Algebraic and Geometric Preliminaries

1.1 Finite Fields

Let p be a large prime (\approx 2^{254}). The field \mathbb{F}_p consists of integers modulo p with addition and multiplication defined modulo p. Every non‑zero element has a multiplicative inverse.
We denote the field size by |\mathbb{F}_p| = p.

1.2 Elliptic Curves and Bilinear Pairings

An elliptic curve over \mathbb{F}_p is the set of solutions (x,y) \in \mathbb{F}_p^2 to

y^2 = x^3 + a x + b, \qquad 4a^3 + 27b^2 \neq 0,
\]  

together with a point at infinity \mathcal{O}. The points form an abelian group under the chord‑and‑tangent rule.

Let \mathbb{G}_1, \mathbb{G}_2 be two cyclic subgroups of an elliptic curve group of the same prime order r, and let \mathbb{G}_T be a multiplicative group of order r.

A bilinear pairing is a map e: \mathbb{G}_1 \times \mathbb{G}_2 \to \mathbb{G}_T satisfying:

1. Bilinearity: e(aP, bQ) = e(P,Q)^{ab} for all a,b \in \mathbb{F}_r, P \in \mathbb{G}_1, Q \in \mathbb{G}_2.
2. Non‑degeneracy: e(P,Q) \neq 1_{\mathbb{G}_T} for some P,Q.
3. Efficient computability.

We work with the BN254 curve (Barreto–Naehrig, embedding degree 12).
Security note: The curve was originally targeted at 128‑bit security, but due to recent improvements in the number field sieve for pairing‑friendly curves, its effective security is now estimated around 100–110 bits. For applications requiring a strict 128‑bit margin, BLS12‑381 would be a more appropriate choice. The pairing used is the optimal Ate pairing.

