2. Zero‑Knowledge Proofs: R1CS and Groth16

2.1 Rank‑1 Constraint Systems (R1CS)

Let \mathbb{F} be a finite field. An R1CS instance consists of three matrices A,B,C \in \mathbb{F}^{n \times m} and a witness vector \mathbf{w} \in \mathbb{F}^m such that

(A\mathbf{w}) \circ (B\mathbf{w}) = C\mathbf{w},
\]  

where \circ denotes the Hadamard (entry‑wise) product.
The first component of \mathbf{w} is fixed to 1 to encode constant terms.

Encoding arithmetic gates.

· Addition: w_k = w_i + w_j.
    Set row k of A with 1 at positions i and j, all other entries 0; row k of B with 1 at position 1 (the constant wire); row k of C with 1 at position k.
    Then (A\mathbf{w})_k = w_i + w_j, (B\mathbf{w})_k = 1, so the Hadamard product forces C\mathbf{w}_k = w_k = w_i + w_j.
· Multiplication: w_k = w_i \times w_j.
    Set A_{k,i}=1, B_{k,j}=1, C_{k,k}=1, all other entries 0.
    Then (A\mathbf{w})_k = w_i, (B\mathbf{w})_k = w_j, (C\mathbf{w})_k = w_k, enforcing w_k = w_i w_j.

2.2 Quadratic Arithmetic Programs (QAP)

An R1CS instance can be transformed into a QAP over \mathbb{F}. Choose distinct roots r_1,\dots,r_n \in \mathbb{F}.
There exist polynomials A_i(x), B_i(x), C_i(x) of degree <n such that for all i, A_i(r_k) = A_{k,i}, etc.
Let t(x) = \prod_{k=1}^n (x - r_k) be the vanishing polynomial.

The witness \mathbf{w} satisfies the R1CS iff the polynomial

H(x) = \frac{\left(\sum_{i=0}^m w_i A_i(x)\right)\! \left(\sum_{i=0}^m w_i B_i(x)\right) - \sum_{i=0}^m w_i C_i(x)}{t(x)}
\]  

is a polynomial (i.e. no remainder).

2.3 Groth16 Protocol

We use the Groth16 zk‑SNARK with a common reference string (CRS) in the pairing‑based setting.

Setup (\lambda, C) \to (pk, vk)

· Sample random \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta, \tau \in \mathbb{F}_r^*.
· Compute the CRS elements in \mathbb{G}_1 and \mathbb{G}_2 (written additively):
    [\sigma]_1 = \sigma \cdot g_1, [\sigma]_2 = \sigma \cdot g_2 for relevant scalars.
· The public parameters include:
  [\alpha]_1,\; [\beta]_1,\; [\beta]_2,\; [\delta]_1,\; [\delta]_2,\; \big\{[\tau^i]_1\big\}_{i=0}^{n-1},\; \big\{[\tau^i]_2\big\}_{i=0}^{n-1},
  \]  
  \Big[\frac{\beta A_i(\tau) + \alpha B_i(\tau) + C_i(\tau)}{\gamma}\Big]_1,\;
  \Big[\frac{\beta A_i(\tau) + \alpha B_i(\tau) + C_i(\tau)}{\delta}\Big]_1.
  \]  

Prove (pk, \mathbf{w}) \to \pi = (A, B, C)

· Let w_i be the witness (with w_0=1).
· Compute h(x) = H(x) from the QAP.
· Sample random r,s \in \mathbb{F}_r^*.
· Compute:
  A = \alpha + \sum_{i=0}^m w_i A_i(\tau) + r\delta \quad \in \mathbb{G}_1,
  \]  
  B = \beta + \sum_{i=0}^m w_i B_i(\tau) + s\delta \quad \in \mathbb{G}_2,
  \]  
  C = \frac{\sum_{i=\ell+1}^m w_i(\beta A_i(\tau) + \alpha B_i(\tau) + C_i(\tau)) + h(\tau)t(\tau)}{\delta} + sA + rB - rs\delta \quad \in \mathbb{G}_1.
  \]  
  The sum over i=\ell+1 excludes the public inputs i=1..\ell.

Verify (vk, \pi, \text{public\_inputs}) \to \{0,1\}

· Check:
  e(A, B) = e([\alpha]_1, [\beta]_2) \cdot e\!\left( \sum_{i=0}^\ell a_i \Big[\frac{\beta A_i(\tau) + \alpha B_i(\tau) + C_i(\tau)}{\gamma}\Big]_1,\; [\gamma]_2 \right) \cdot e(C, [\delta]_2).
  \]  

Security (sketch): Perfect completeness holds by algebraic substitution. Knowledge soundness holds under the q‑SDH and q‑PKE assumptions in the generic group model. Zero‑knowledge follows from the randomisers r,s that perfectly blind the witness.

