3. Cryptographic Building Blocks

3.1 Poseidon Hash

We use the Poseidon permutation over \mathbb{F}_p with state size t=3 (typical configuration: 8 full rounds and 57 partial rounds). It is a sponge construction built from a sequence of full and partial rounds of an S‑box x \mapsto x^5, linear mixing with an MDS matrix, and round constants. The final output is a single field element. Poseidon is collision‑resistant and efficiently representable in arithmetic circuits.

3.2 Merkle Trees

A Merkle tree commits to a list of elements with a public root. A Merkle proof for element x at index i consists of the siblings along the path to the root. Verification computes the root using the provided siblings and the hash function. With tree depth d, the proof size is O(d).

