5. Sybil Resistance: Nullifier Uniqueness

Definition (Nullifier).
For a participant with secret key sk and epoch identifier e, the nullifier is

\nu = \text{Poseidon}\big( sk,\; \text{Poseidon}(sk, \text{ctx}),\; e \big).
\]  

Theorem 5.1 (Nullifier Uniqueness per Key per Epoch).
Fix an epoch and a context. If two active nullifiers \nu_i, \nu_j in the sorted list satisfy \nu_i = \nu_j, then either sk_i = sk_j, or a collision in Poseidon has been found. The probability that an adversary can produce two distinct secret keys yielding the same nullifier is bounded by

\Pr[\text{collision}] \le \frac{N^2}{2p} + \epsilon_{\text{hash}}(\lambda),
\]  

where N is the number of identities, p = |\mathbb{F}_p|, and \epsilon_{\text{hash}} is a negligible function in the security parameter.

Proof.
Assume \nu_i = \nu_j. By construction \nu_i = H(sk_i, h_i, e) where h_i = H(sk_i, \text{ctx}), similarly for j. If sk_i \neq sk_j, then a collision has been found in H. Since Poseidon is collision‑resistant, this happens with probability at most \epsilon_{\text{hash}}.
If sk_i = sk_j, then the same key was used twice. The aggregation circuit’s monotonicity check requires \nu_1 < \nu_2 < \dots, so any duplicate (which are equal, not strictly increasing) will cause the proof to be invalid. Hence no two submissions with the same secret key can appear in a valid aggregation proof.
The birthday bound N^2/(2p) accounts for the probability of accidental collision among distinct honest keys, which is added to the adversarial advantage \epsilon_{\text{hash}}. ∎

Corollary 5.2 (Key‑Limited Sybil Resistance).
The system guarantees that a single secret key cannot be used more than once per epoch. This is not full Sybil resistance: an adversary can still generate k distinct secret keys and obtain k valid nullifiers. Full Sybil resistance requires an external identity‑binding mechanism (stake, proof‑of‑personhood, biometric oracle, etc.) that limits the number of secret keys a real‑world entity can control.

