6. Softmax Allocation and Sensitivity

The PRI mechanism distributes a fixed total resource R each epoch according to impact scores \{s_i\} via a softmax function.

Definition 6.1 (PRI Allocation).
Given scores s_i \in [0,1], temperature \beta > 0, the allocation to participant i is

P_i = R \cdot \frac{\exp(\beta s_i)}{\sum_{j=1}^N \exp(\beta s_j)}.
\]  

Define the normalised probability p_i = \exp(\beta s_i)/\sum_j \exp(\beta s_j).

Lemma 6.2 (Bounded Influence).
For any i,j,

\left|\frac{\partial P_i}{\partial s_j}\right| \le \beta\, P_i \le \beta R,
\]  

and the relative sensitivity is

\left|\frac{\partial P_i}{P_i \,\partial s_j}\right| = \beta\,|\delta_{ij} - p_j| \le \beta.
\]  

Proof.
The gradient of the softmax is standard:

\frac{\partial p_i}{\partial s_j} = \beta\, p_i (\delta_{ij} - p_j).
\]  

Since P_i = R\, p_i, we have

\frac{\partial P_i}{\partial s_j} = \beta\, P_i (\delta_{ij} - p_j).
\]  

Taking absolute values, |\delta_{ij} - p_j| \le \max(p_j, 1-p_j) \le 1, so |\partial P_i/\partial s_j| \le \beta P_i \le \beta R. The relative change is obtained by dividing by P_i, giving \beta |\delta_{ij}-p_j| \le \beta. ∎

This shows that a small manipulation of one’s own score (\delta_{ii}=1) can change one’s allocation by at most a factor \beta relative to its current size, limiting the profitability of undetected score inflation.

