7. Incentive Mechanism: Repeated Game Analysis

We model the interactions of N participants as an infinitely repeated game with discount factor \delta \in (0,1). Each epoch is a stage game with the following Prisoner’s Dilemma payoff matrix (row player payoff / column player payoff):

 Cooperate (C) Defect (D)
C (2,2) (0,3)
D (3,0) (1,1)

These numbers represent normalised utility from the PRI allocation net of effort cost.
In a single shot, (D,D) is the unique Nash equilibrium, while (C,C) yields higher total welfare.

We consider grim trigger strategies: start by cooperating, and if any player defects, switch to D forever.

Lemma 7.1 (Punishment Equilibrium).
The strategy profile “all D” is a Nash equilibrium of the stage game: deviating to C gives payoff 0 < 1, so no profitable unilateral deviation.

Theorem 7.2 (Cooperation as Subgame Perfect Equilibrium).
Under grim trigger, the cooperative profile (all C every period) is a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium if

\delta \ge \frac{1}{2}.
\]  

Proof.
Consider a player contemplating a unilateral deviation from C in the current period.

· Deviation payoff: playing D while the rest play C yields 3 this period.
· Consequence: from next period onward, all players revert to D, giving a per‑period payoff of 1.

The present value of cooperating forever is:

V_C = 2 + \delta \cdot 2 + \delta^2 \cdot 2 + \dots = \frac{2}{1-\delta}.
\]  

The present value of deviating once and then facing (D,D) forever is:

V_D = 3 + \delta \cdot 1 + \delta^2 \cdot 1 + \dots = 3 + \frac{\delta}{1-\delta}.
\]  

Cooperation is sustainable iff V_C \ge V_D, i.e.

\frac{2}{1-\delta} \ge 3 + \frac{\delta}{1-\delta}.
\]  

Multiply by (1-\delta):

2 \ge 3(1-\delta) + \delta = 3 - 2\delta.
\]  

Rearrange: 2\delta \ge 1, so \delta \ge 1/2.

When \delta \ge 1/2, no player has an incentive to deviate from the cooperative path, and since the punishment is itself an equilibrium (Lemma 7.1), the strategy profile is subgame perfect. ∎

Remark.
The threshold can be adjusted for different cost structures. For example, if the cost of cooperating reduces the (C,C) payoff to 1.7 while (D,C) still pays 3 and (D,D) remains 1, the inequality becomes

\frac{1.7}{1-\delta} \ge 3 + \frac{\delta}{1-\delta}
\;\Longrightarrow\; 1.7 \ge 3(1-\delta) + \delta = 3 - 2\delta
\;\Longrightarrow\; 2\delta \ge 1.3 \;\Longrightarrow\; \delta \ge 0.65.
\]  

The derivation is mechanically identical to the proof above; only the numerical value changes.

