8. Complexity Analysis of the Aggregation Circuit

On‑chain (circuit) constraints:
The aggregation circuit must enforce N-1 inequality checks \nu_i < \nu_{i+1}. Each inequality over \mathbb{F}_p is implemented by bit‑decomposing the two elements and comparing bitwise, requiring O(\log p) addition/multiplication gates. Hence total gates = O(N \log p), which is linear in N for fixed field size.

Off‑chain prover work:
The prover sorts the nullifier list in O(N \log N) time using a standard sorting algorithm. This is performed locally and does not increase circuit complexity.

Comparison with naive on‑chain sorting:
A circuit that sorted N elements would require a sorting network of O(N \log N) comparators, each comparator O(\log p) gates, leading to O(N \log N \log p) constraints. By shifting sorting off‑chain, we reduce on‑chain work by a factor of \log N, a significant improvement for large N.

