9. Overall System Soundness

Theorem 9.1 (End‑to‑End Soundness).
Assume the cryptographic primitives (Poseidon, Groth16) are secure. Then in any valid epoch finalisation, the aggregated impact score \mu is computed from a set of distinct secret‑key‑per‑task contributions whose individual task‑completion and impact‑assessment proofs were valid.

Proof sketch.

· Each individual proof (task completion, impact assessment) is verified on‑chain before the aggregation proof is submitted. The Groth16 knowledge‑soundness ensures that valid proofs imply the existence of witnesses satisfying the respective circuit constraints.
· The aggregation circuit receives the previously verified nullifier–score pairs as private inputs and enforces: (i) the nullifiers are strictly increasing, (ii) \mu equals the arithmetic mean of the scores, (iii) the commitment C binds the whole batch.
· By Theorem 5.1, strict monotonicity implies that all nullifiers are distinct, hence no secret key was used more than once.
· Thus the aggregation correctly summarises the set of verified contributions. ∎

