Appendix A: Detailed Cryptographic Primitives

A.1 Poseidon Hash Construction

The Poseidon permutation is defined over a finite field \mathbb{F}_p. We use a state size t = 3 (two elements for the rate, one for the capacity in the sponge construction).

Permutation structure.
The permutation consists of R_F = 8 full rounds (where the S‑box is applied to every element of the state) and R_P = 57 partial rounds (where the S‑box is applied to only one element). A round r comprises:

1. AddConstants: x_i \leftarrow x_i + c_i^{(r)}, where c^{(r)} are round‑specific constants.
2. SubWords: For full rounds: x_i \leftarrow x_i^5 for all i.
      For partial rounds: x_0 \leftarrow x_0^5; other x_i unchanged.
3. MixLayer: Multiply the state vector by a t \times t MDS matrix M over \mathbb{F}_p.

The MDS matrix ensures that the linear layer provides full diffusion. The constant round numbers are chosen to resist algebraic and statistical attacks.

Sponge mode.
The hash function \text{Poseidon}(m_1,\dots,m_k) operates as follows:

· Initialise the state with all zeros.
· For each message element m_i, add it to the first rate element (element 0) and apply the permutation.
· After all elements are absorbed, apply a final permutation and output the first rate element.

The sponge construction gives collision resistance and indifferentiability from a random oracle when the capacity is large enough (1 field element provides ≈ 254 bits of capacity, more than sufficient).

A.2 Merkle Tree and Proof Verification

A Merkle tree of depth d stores up to 2^d leaves. Each leaf is a field element. The hash function for internal nodes is \text{Poseidon}(L, R).

Merkle proof.
A proof for leaf x at index i consists of the sibling hashes h_1, \dots, h_d and a direction bit for each (0 = left, 1 = right). The verifier recomputes:

h_0 = \text{leaf hash}(x),\qquad h_{j} = \begin{cases} 
\text{Poseidon}(h_{j-1}, s_j) & \text{if direction } 0\\
\text{Poseidon}(s_j, h_{j-1}) & \text{if direction } 1
\end{cases}
\]  

and checks that h_d equals the public root.

In the circuit, each Poseidon call costs ≈ 275 multiplication gates (for the chosen round numbers). A tree of depth d thus contributes d hashes, i.e. 275d constraints.

A.3 Bilinear Pairing Details

BN254 curve.
The curve is defined by the equation y^2 = x^3 + 3 over \mathbb{F}_p with

p = 36 t^4 + 36 t^3 + 24 t^2 + 6 t + 1,\qquad t = 4965661367192848881.
\]  

The subgroup order r is a 254‑bit prime. The embedding degree is 12, so the full extension tower \mathbb{F}_{p^{12}} is used for the target group.

Optimal Ate pairing.
The pairing is computed as e(P,Q) = f_{u,Q}(P)^{(p^{12}-1)/r} where u = 6t+2 is the BN parameter and f_{u,Q} is a Miller function. The algorithm runs in about  \log_2(u)  Miller steps. For the Groth16 verifier, the pairing is called three times (or can be reduced with a single product check if using a final exponentiation optimisation).

