Appendix B: R1CS and QAP Transformation

B.1 From Arithmetic Circuit to R1CS

Any arithmetic circuit with multiplication gates and linear combination gates can be expressed as an R1CS. Each gate adds one row to the matrices A,B,C. The transformation is:

· For each input and intermediate signal, assign a wire index w_i.
· For addition of wires i and j into k: set A_{k,i}=1, A_{k,j}=1, B_{k,0}=1, C_{k,k}=1.
· For multiplication: set A_{k,i}=1, B_{k,j}=1, C_{k,k}=1.
· For constants c: use the wire w_0=1 with coefficient c.

This encoding satisfies (A\mathbf{w})_k \cdot (B\mathbf{w})_k = (C\mathbf{w})_k for each gate k.

B.2 QAP via Lagrange Interpolation

Let n be the number of gates. Choose n distinct evaluation points r_1,\dots,r_n \in \mathbb{F}. For each wire index i, define polynomials A_i(x) of degree <n such that A_i(r_k) = A_{k,i}. This is done by Lagrange interpolation:

A_i(x) = \sum_{k=1}^n A_{k,i} \cdot L_k(x),\quad L_k(x) = \prod_{j \neq k} \frac{x - r_j}{r_k - r_j}.
\]  

Similarly define B_i(x) and C_i(x).

Define the vanishing polynomial t(x) = \prod_{k=1}^n (x - r_k).

B.3 Equivalence Proof

Lemma B.1. A vector \mathbf{w} satisfies the R1CS if and only if the polynomial

P(x) = \left(\sum_i w_i A_i(x)\right)\left(\sum_i w_i B_i(x)\right) - \sum_i w_i C_i(x)
\]  

is divisible by t(x).

Proof.
For each gate k, P(r_k) = (A\mathbf{w})_k \cdot (B\mathbf{w})_k - (C\mathbf{w})_k. So P(r_k)=0 for all k iff all constraints are satisfied. Since t(x) has roots exactly \{r_k\}, P(x) is divisible by t(x) iff P(r_k)=0 for all k. ∎

Thus the quotient H(x) = P(x)/t(x) is a polynomial of degree at most n-2 (since the product of two degree‑(n-1) polynomials is degree 2n-2, minus degree n-1 terms yields degree <2n; division by degree n polynomial gives degree <n).

