Appendix E: Softmax Allocation – Extended Analysis

E.1 Gradient Derivation

We have p_i = \frac{e^{\beta s_i}}{Z}, with Z = \sum_j e^{\beta s_j}.

\frac{\partial p_i}{\partial s_j} = \frac{\partial}{\partial s_j} \left( e^{\beta s_i} Z^{-1} \right) 
= \beta \delta_{ij} p_i - e^{\beta s_i} \cdot \frac{\beta e^{\beta s_j}}{Z^2} 
= \beta p_i (\delta_{ij} - p_j).
\]  

The resource allocation is P_i = R p_i, so \frac{\partial P_i}{\partial s_j} = R \beta p_i (\delta_{ij} - p_j) = \beta P_i (\delta_{ij} - p_j).

E.2 Lipschitz Continuity

The function f(s) = (P_1, \dots, P_N) is Lipschitz continuous in the L_1 norm with constant 2\beta R. For any two score vectors s, s':

\sum_i |P_i(s) - P_i(s')| \le 2\beta R \sum_i |s_i - s_i'|.
\]  

This can be derived from the bound on partial derivatives and the mean value theorem.

E.3 Effect of Temperature \beta

· As \beta \to 0, the allocation becomes uniform: P_i \to R/N.
· As \beta \to \infty, the entire resource goes to the participant(s) with the maximum score.
· The sensitivity bound \beta R quantifies the maximum change in allocation per unit score change, providing a tunable resistance to manipulation.

