Appendix H: Implementation Considerations

H.1 BN254 vs BLS12‑381

Property BN254 BLS12‑381
Field size p ≈ 254 bits ≈ 381 bits
Security level ≈ 100–110 bits ≈ 128 bits
Pairing efficiency Slightly faster Slower (larger field)
Curve maturity Widely used (Ethereum) Becoming standard
Proof size Smaller (due to smaller field) Larger

For applications where long‑term security is paramount (e.g., value transfer), BLS12‑381 is recommended despite larger circuits and proofs.

H.2 Batch Verification

Multiple Groth16 proofs sharing the same CRS can be batch‑verified by combining pairing equations using a random linear combination. This reduces verification cost from O(k) pairings to O(1) pairings and some exponentiations, at the cost of a small soundness loss.

H.3 Recursive Proof Composition

For large N, proving the aggregation circuit as a single monolithic circuit becomes expensive. A recursive proof composition (using Halo2, Nova, or a folding scheme) can split the aggregation into a tree of smaller proofs, each covering a chunk of nullifiers. This reduces prover memory and allows parallel proving, while the final verifier checks a single constant‑size proof.

